WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.842 --> 00:00:03.280
A lathe makes the same shaft all day.

2
00:00:03.964 --> 00:00:09.096
With SPC, statistical process control, you watch that process while it runs,

3
00:00:09.224 --> 00:00:12.057
so you see a change before it makes bad parts.

4
00:00:13.097 --> 00:00:18.670
These shafts must be 20 millimetres in diameter, plus or minus five hundredths.

5
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.579
Every hour the operator takes a subgroup: 5 shafts made one after another.

6
00:00:25.331 --> 00:00:26.631
He measures all five.

7
00:00:27.642 --> 00:00:30.881
The result goes on the control chart next to the machine:

8
00:00:31.078 --> 00:00:32.890
a measurement plotted over time,

9
00:00:33.075 --> 00:00:35.687
with a centre line and two control limits.

10
00:00:36.977 --> 00:00:39.531
The classic is the X-bar and R chart.

11
00:00:40.346 --> 00:00:43.237
At the top the average of the five, the X-bar.

12
00:00:43.961 --> 00:00:47.305
At the bottom the range: the largest minus the smallest.

13
00:00:48.359 --> 00:00:52.864
X-bar shows whether the centre moves; the range whether the spread changes.

14
00:00:53.886 --> 00:00:58.739
The control limits come from the process itself: three sigma of the averages.

15
00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:01.736
From 20.000,

16
00:01:01.945 --> 00:01:13.520
an average range of 0.020 and the table constant 0.577 follows 19.9885 to 20.0115.

17
00:01:14.497 --> 00:01:16.900
Spec limits are what the customer needs,

18
00:01:17.098 --> 00:01:19.838
control limits what the process normally does.

19
00:01:20.534 --> 00:01:24.795
Spec stays off this chart: averages spread less than single shafts.

20
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.900
Hour by hour the chart fills.

21
00:01:28.644 --> 00:01:38.129
At ten o’clock 20.002, then 20.004, 20.005 and 20.006.

22
00:01:39.194 --> 00:01:46.520
Then 20.008, 20.009, and at four o’clock 20.011.

23
00:01:47.245 --> 00:01:50.426
All inside the limits, and far inside spec.

24
00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.164
But seven rising in a row hardly ever happens by chance.

25
00:01:55.856 --> 00:01:59.722
The rule says: six or more points in a row that keep rising.

26
00:02:00.429 --> 00:02:02.774
This is a trend, and the rule fires.

27
00:02:03.904 --> 00:02:08.292
Other rules: one point outside a limit, seven in a row on one side,

28
00:02:08.478 --> 00:02:10.289
or two of three beyond two sigma.

29
00:02:11.055 --> 00:02:13.714
Some plants use eight or nine in a row.

30
00:02:14.412 --> 00:02:18.023
More rules catch more, but also give more false alarms.

31
00:02:19.388 --> 00:02:24.067
Every chart needs a reaction plan: what does the operator do when a rule fires,

32
00:02:24.275 --> 00:02:25.273
and who does he call?

33
00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:29.330
He stops and checks the insert, the cutting tip.

34
00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:30.420
Worn.

35
00:02:31.124 --> 00:02:33.446
At ten past four a new one is in.

36
00:02:34.471 --> 00:02:38.174
Then the suspect parts: everything since the last good check.

37
00:02:38.934 --> 00:02:40.397
They are held and checked.

38
00:02:41.467 --> 00:02:43.406
Not one shaft out of spec.

39
00:02:44.143 --> 00:02:46.674
A chart nobody reacts to is wallpaper.

40
00:02:48.045 --> 00:02:49.450
Now a stable process.

41
00:02:50.132 --> 00:02:53.174
The operator measures every shaft and after every

42
00:02:53.255 --> 00:02:56.204
part moves the offset by that part’s deviation.

43
00:02:57.218 --> 00:02:58.240
That is tampering.

44
00:02:58.965 --> 00:03:04.537
Each deviation is mostly noise, so correcting for it adds a second noise on top.

45
00:03:05.564 --> 00:03:08.176
On the chart: a zig-zag from part to part,

46
00:03:08.362 --> 00:03:10.939
and more spread than the gauge study explains.

47
00:03:11.952 --> 00:03:12.881
He means well.

48
00:03:13.628 --> 00:03:15.543
I keep it on nominal, he says.

49
00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:20.736
The funnel experiment, made famous by Deming, shows why.

50
00:03:21.468 --> 00:03:24.127
Marbles drop through a funnel onto a target.

51
00:03:25.199 --> 00:03:26.592
If the funnel stays still,

52
00:03:26.801 --> 00:03:30.435
they land in a cloud around the target: the ordinary spread.

53
00:03:31.486 --> 00:03:34.226
Move it after every drop to correct the miss,

54
00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:38.371
and the standard deviation rises by about 41 percent.

55
00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:40.662
The variance doubles.

56
00:03:41.369 --> 00:03:44.666
So does the operator who adjusts after every part.

57
00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:47.675
Explain it this way, not as blame.

58
00:03:48.989 --> 00:03:51.996
The rule: adjust only on a signal from the chart.

59
00:03:52.768 --> 00:03:55.078
In between, leave the machine alone.

60
00:03:56.097 --> 00:03:59.313
Known wear is handled by wear compensation: a planned,

61
00:03:59.487 --> 00:04:02.250
fixed offset after a set number of parts.

62
00:04:03.262 --> 00:04:05.154
If tampering was the main source,

63
00:04:05.305 --> 00:04:11.331
the standard deviation drops by roughly 30 percent after two weeks; exactly 29.

64
00:04:12.335 --> 00:04:14.738
An in-machine gauge that sets the offset

65
00:04:14.773 --> 00:04:17.362
must adjust on the average of several parts,

66
00:04:17.513 --> 00:04:18.895
never on one reading.

67
00:04:20.212 --> 00:04:22.858
If you can only count, there are four charts.

68
00:04:23.556 --> 00:04:28.547
The p chart plots the fraction defective, even when the sample size varies.

69
00:04:29.546 --> 00:04:33.993
The np chart counts the defective parts in a sample of fixed size.

70
00:04:35.009 --> 00:04:37.819
If you count defects, such as scratches per panel,

71
00:04:37.969 --> 00:04:43.263
use a c chart for units of the same size, and a u chart when the size varies.

72
00:04:44.285 --> 00:04:45.841
Count charts react late.

73
00:04:46.578 --> 00:04:49.527
At 1 percent defective and samples of 50,

74
00:04:49.736 --> 00:04:54.368
the upper limit is about 5.2 percent: two bad parts stay inside.

75
00:04:55.084 --> 00:04:56.303
So measure if you can.

76
00:04:57.611 --> 00:04:59.887
Finally: what goes into one subgroup?

77
00:05:00.657 --> 00:05:02.248
A mould with 4 cavities.

78
00:05:02.958 --> 00:05:06.778
Every hour one part from each cavity goes on the chart together.

79
00:05:07.771 --> 00:05:11.300
The R chart is wide and the X-bar chart never signals,

80
00:05:11.463 --> 00:05:13.762
yet cavity 3 makes thin walls.

81
00:05:14.454 --> 00:05:17.542
The range here measures the difference between the cavities.

82
00:05:18.585 --> 00:05:23.253
A rational subgroup is made of parts made close together, from one cavity.

83
00:05:23.926 --> 00:05:25.772
So a chart for each cavity.

84
00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:32.852
Work out the limits from 20 to 25 subgroups in a stable period, then freeze them.
